-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Occlusion:** If a query point is occluded in some views, the pixel-aligned features $p^x_{c,t}$ may be noisy [S1, unofficial]. The attention mechanism mitigates this by down-weighting incompatible views, but extreme occlusion may require robust skeletal priors [S1, unofficial].
-*   **SMPL Accuracy:** The skeletal feature retrieval depends on the accuracy of the SMPL pose estimation [S1, unofficial]. Errors in SMPL pose will propagate to $s^0_{c,t}$, potentially misleading the attention weights [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Trilinear Interpolation:** Sampling from the SparseConvNet volume requires careful handling of boundaries outside the SMPL mesh [G]. Points outside the SMPL volume should be handled with a fallback or clamping to avoid NaNs [G].
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls**
+*   **Occlusion:** Pixel-aligned features may be occluded. The attention mechanism helps mitigate this by weighting skeletal features (which are geometrically consistent) higher when image features are unreliable [S1, unofficial].
+*   **SMPL Space Alignment:** Accurate trilinear interpolation requires the query point $\mathbf{x}$ to be correctly mapped to the SMPL space. Misalignment leads to incorrect skeletal features [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Augmentation:** Skeletal features are time-augmented, meaning they capture temporal dynamics. Ensure $t$ is consistent between skeletal and image feature retrieval [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [SparseConvNet-based Skeletal Feature Sampling](16444-sparseconvnet-based-skeletal-feature-sampling.md)
-This method samples time-augmented skeletal features at arbitrary 3D query points by diffusing discrete skeletal features into a continuous 3D volume using sparse convolutions. It differs from dense voxelization by processing only occupied voxels, significantly improving efficiency for large 3D spaces. It is chosen when computational efficiency is critical and the scene contains large empty regions. See [SparseConvNet-based Skeletal Feature Sampling](16444-sparseconvnet-based-skeletal-feature-sampling.md).
+This method samples time-augmented skeletal features at arbitrary 3D query points by diffusing discrete skeletal features into a continuous 3D volume using sparse convolutions, enabling spatially aware feature retrieval for NeRF input. It differs from dense voxelization by using sparse convolutions to process only occupied voxels, improving efficiency for large 3D spaces.
 
 ### Multi-view Pose Refinement
-A technique to improve pose refinement by incorporating multi-view constraints when motion is captured from multiple cameras, averaging joint rotation estimates without requiring camera calibration [S2, unofficial]. Rotations are relative to the parent and root, allowing averaging without calibrating cameras; only global position and orientation remain view-specific [S2, unofficial]. Initialize $\theta_k$ by averaging individual joint rotation estimates from all $V$ views $v \in [1, \dots, V]$ [S2, unofficial]. The multi-view loss function is $L_{MV}(\theta, \phi) = \sum_v \sum_k \left( d(C_\phi(\theta_k, \omega_{k,0}^{(v)}, \omega_{k,1}^{(v)}), I_k) + \lambda_\theta \|\theta_k - \hat{\theta}_k\|^2 + \lambda_t \|\partial^2 \theta_k / \partial t^2\|^2 \right)$, where $\theta_k$ is shared across all views $v$, except for $\omega_{k,0}$ and $\omega_{k,1}$ which are estimated independently per camera view $v$ [S2, unofficial].
+A technique to improve pose refinement by incorporating multi-view constraints when motion is captured from multiple cameras, averaging joint rotation estimates without requiring camera calibration [S2, unofficial]. Rotations are relative to the parent and root, allowing averaging without calibrating cameras. Only global position and orientation remain view-specific [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Step:** Initialize $\theta_k$ by averaging individual joint rotation estimates from all $V$ views $v \in [1, ..., V]$ [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Formula:** Multi-view loss function:
+    $$ L_{MV}(\theta, \phi) = \sum_v \sum_k \left( d(C_\phi(\theta_k, \omega_{k,0}^{(v)}, \omega_{k,1}^{(v)}), I_k) + \lambda_\theta \|\theta_k - \hat{\theta}_k\|^2 + \lambda_t \|\frac{\partial^2 \theta_k}{\partial t^2}\|^2 \right) $$
+    where $\theta_k$ is shared across all views $v$, except for $\omega_{k,0}$ and $\omega_{k,1}$ which are estimated independently per camera view $v$ since the relative camera position and orientation is unknown [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Dominated by the multi-view attention mechanism, which is $O(C^2 \cdot d)$ for the attention weights and $O(C \cdot d)$ for the aggregation, plus the MLP inference [G]. The SparseConvNet sampling is efficient due to sparsity [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the SparseConvNet volume for skeletal features and the image features for all views [S1, unofficial]. The attention matrix is $C \times C$ [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Effectively combines geometric priors (SMPL) with visual details (images) [S1, unofficial]. Handles multi-view inconsistencies via attention [S1, unofficial]. Improves rendering quality for articulated humans [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Relies on accurate SMPL pose estimation [S1, unofficial]. Computationally more expensive than standard NeRF due to multi-view processing and attention [G].
-*   **When to use:** Dynamic human rendering where multi-view consistency and geometric priors are beneficial [G].
-*   **When not to use:** Static scenes without articulated subjects, or resource-constrained environments where SMPL inference and multi-view attention are too costly [G].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Dominated by the attention mechanism $O(C^2 \cdot d)$ and the MLPs $O(d \cdot L_{mlp})$. SparseConvNet interpolation is efficient for sparse data.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the SparseConvNet volume and image features for all views and times.
+*   **Strengths:** Robust to occlusions and ambiguous geometry due to skeletal prior. Effective for human performance rendering.
+*   **Limitations:** Requires a parametric human model (SMPL). Performance depends on the accuracy of the SMPL fit and camera poses.
+*   **When to Use:** Human-centric scenes with articulated motion.
+*   **When Not to Use:** Static scenes or scenes without a parametric model prior.
 
 ## Background
-The method originates from the "Neural Human Performer" paper, which introduces a framework for learning generalizable radiance fields for human performance rendering [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the idea of using skeletal priors (SMPL) to regularize the radiance field, extending it with multi-view aggregation to handle dynamic scenes and multiple viewpoints [S1, unofficial]. The multi-view pose refinement variant is described in "A-NeRF," which focuses on articulated neural radiance fields for learning human shape, appearance, and pose, incorporating multi-view constraints for pose refinement [S2, unofficial].
+Originated in "Neural Human Performer" [S1, unofficial] and "A-NeRF" [S2, unofficial]. It builds on the idea of using parametric models to regularize neural radiance fields for humans. The method was developed to address the challenges of rendering high-fidelity human performances from multi-view videos.
 
